    "processes": ["derivation", "sound-change"],
    "headline": "Hotoke, “Buddha”, most likely comes from Proto-Koreanic *Pwutukye, related to modern Korean 부처 (Bucheo).",
    "morphemes": [],
    "sources": [
      {
        "quote": "*⟨poto₂ke₂⟩ → */pətəkəi̯/ → /ɸotoke/ → /hotoke/"
      },
      {
        "quote": "From Old Japanese, most likely from Proto-Koreanic *Pwutukye, hence related to modern Korean 부처 (Bucheo), Manchu ᡶᡠᠴᡳᡥᡳ (fucihi); see the Proto-Korean entry for more. The word was presumably disseminated by the Baekje people of southwestern Korea, who first introduced Buddhism to Japan. The first two syllables are ultimately from Sanskrit बुद्ध (buddhá), via Middle Chinese 佛陀 (MC bjut da) or some similar Sinitic form. Thomas Pellard speculates that the final element is an ancient Koreanic word for \"king; lord\"."
      },
      {
        "quote": "The more traditional explanation still given in Japanese dictionaries is that the final ke element might be the Chinese-derived suffix 気(け) (ke, “spirit”), but a Koreanic explanation is more plausible."
      },
      {
        "quote": "First attested in the Nihon Shoki of 720 CE."
      }
    ],
    "otherReadings": [
      {
        "readings": ["ぶつ"],
        "quotes": [
          "From Middle Chinese 佛 (MC bjut), shortened from 佛陀 (MC bjut da), itself a transliteration of Sanskrit बुद्ध (buddha)."
        ]
      },
      {
